    "created_at": "2023-03-09T23:22:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-03-09T23:22:12Z",
    "body": "Thank you all for attending today! Here are the notes from this meeting (the next one is at https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9002):\r\n\r\n### Agenda\r\n\r\n1. Review past action items\r\n    1. Marcos & Anne to kindly look for ways the proposed solution [here](https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/586#issuecomment-1426042297) might run into problems.\r\n        1. Anne commented slightly, discussion will continue async.\r\n    1. Feedback from everyone is welcome on Anne's PR to [Give ElementInternals element-reflecting attributes more power](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/8932), will discuss [this](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8544) again next time.\r\n        1. Mason took a look and is in spirit supportive.\r\n        1. Olli will take a look.\r\n1. Carryovers from last time\r\n    1. [Mason] [Add DOMParser option to parse declarative shadow DOM](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8759)\r\n        1. Both Mozilla and Chromium representatives believe adding such an option to DOMParser is a reasonable path forward for the whole-document case.\r\n        1. No need to support XHR since it's a legacy API. DOMParser has no replacement now.\r\n1. New topics\r\n    1. [Connected to DOMParser discussion] setHTML: entry point to the sanitizer, or a generic HTML setter?\r\n        1. Question: are we OK adding a sanitizing method where sanitizing cannot be turned off?\r\n            1. Daniel: this is very important\r\n            1. Olli: no strong opinions. Simon: OK with this. Freddy seemed to be warming up to this idea.\r\n        1. Question: do we want to add a non-sanitizing method?\r\n            1. Mason: This is necessary for key DSD use cases, like script-containing custom elements.\r\n            1. Olli: This is also important for performance.\r\n        1. Question: do we want to add the variant methods (e.g. [unsanitized, sanitized] x [prepend|append|set] x [XML|HTML])?\r\n            1. Mason/Daniel: the [unsanitized] branch of this seems like adding more dangerous things, but OK to compromise if other people really want them\r\n            1. Some discussion of using options bags to reduce the number of methods, but nobody really in favor.\r\n            1. Simon: we can defer the [XML] branch, especially since we don't know how DSD will work there anyway.\r\n        1. Future question: how do we support sanitization in a future streaming HTML parser API? (First we need to design the streaming HTML parser API...)\r\n            1. Simon: This would need fundamental changes to the sanitizer spec, working on the tree builder instead.\r\n            1. Daniel: This seems doable, even if a lot of work. Pointers from Simon appreciated.\r\n        1. Discussion will continue async!!\r\n\r\n### Action Items\r\n\r\n1. @zcorpan to open an issue about streaming sanitized parsing, with pointers to help @otherdaniel understand how we could build that in the future.\r\n",
